<Layout title="Editorial Policy" description="How Vera Health Notes checks sources, reviews health content, and handles corrections.">
  <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
  <div class="copy">
    <p>We start with a reader’s question and check the sources that can answer it. For health coverage, we look to clinical guidelines, public health agencies, and relevant research. We identify the evidence behind an article and explain what it can support.</p>
    <h2>Review before publication</h2>
    <p>Articles go through editorial and source review. Content requiring clinical review stays in draft until that review is recorded. We identify reviewers and their roles only when we have verified them.</p>
    <h2>Product research and corrections</h2>
    <p>We identify claims made by sellers or manufacturers and say when testing, labels, or purchase terms could not be verified. Any material affiliate relationship is disclosed where it is relevant. When we make a substantive correction, we explain it in the affected article.</p>
    <p>See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a> and <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Layout>
